  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 12:45 AM EST...Current sfc analysis shows that \nthe cold front that brought showers yesterday is located just west of \nthe Capital District. The best upper forcing remains off to our \nwest, but persistent weak low-level convergence along the front\nwithin a moist airmass is leading to a few isolated showers in \nthe vicinity and ahead of the cold front. Behind the front, \ncurrent satellite imagery shows some partial clearing, and given\nthe low-level moisture from yesterday's showers we should see \npatchy fog develop, especially in the more sheltered areas where\nwinds become calm. Otherwise, the rest of the night will be \nunseasonably warm and muggy with lows in the mid 40s to upper \n50s. \n\nLate tonight/early tomorrow morning, increasingly diffluent flow \naloft will lead to an uptick in shower activity along the front as \nit slowly makes its way into western New England. Then, by tomorrow \nafternoon, a southern stream shortwave rounding the base of an upper-\nlevel low (ULL) centered over the southern Great Lakes will induce \nsfc cyclogenesis off the east coasts, with a wave of low \npressure developing along the stalled frontal boundary. Forcing \nlooks impressive, with CVA, the right entrance of the upper jet \nnearby, and increased low-level convergence near the front. The \nimpressive forcing will overlap with deep Atlantic moisture \n(PWATs to 1.5-1.75\") ahead of the front. With some pockets of \nelevated instability, a period of steady moderate to locally \nheavy rain is expected across portions of southern New England \ntomorrow afternoon. The big question is how far west the front \nstalls, and thus how far west the precip shield makes it into \nour region. There will also be a very sharp accumulation \ngradient on the northwestern side of the rain shield as well. \nCurrently, southeastern Litchfield County near Rte 6 has a 50% \nchance to see >1\" of rain according to the latest NBM, but these\nprobabilities drop to <20% northwest of Rte 202. Our current \ndeterministic forecast has some additional light rain reaching \nsoutheastern Dutchess and Berkshire Counties, with most of the \nrest of the region remaining mainly dry but cloudy tomorrow. \nEven if rainfall amounts end up above the current forecast, the \nvery dry antecedent conditions should prevent any flooding \nconcerns. Tomorrow will again be on the warm side with highs\nwell into the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain associated with this coastal low comes to \nan end Friday night. However, the parent ULL will be slowly \ndrifting overhead Saturday into Saturday night. Its exact track \nremains somewhat uncertain, but general consensus still seems to\nbe near or just north of the I-90 corridor. With moisture \nwrapping back around the ULL, fairly widespread showers \noverspread the region from west to east Saturday, lasting into \nSaturday night. An inverted trough at the sfc extending from the\ndeepening coastal low back towards the ULL will result in \nincreased low-level convergence, helping to locally enhance \ncoverage and intensity of showers. Latest 00z guidance has\nticked slightly north with this feature keeping it mainly north\nof I-90. Most areas will see at least an additional quarter to \nhalf inch of rain, with higher amounts of 0.5 to 1 inch possible\nwhere the inverted trough sets up. Lower rainfall amounts closer\nto a tenth of an inch expected closer to I-84, especially if\nnorth trend with the inverted trough continues. Most areas dry \nout Sunday morning, but some lingering precip will be possible \nin the high terrain north of I-90 as the upper deformation axis \npasses just north of our region with some W/NW upslope \nenhancement also expected. \n\nAs the system pulls away from our region, it cooler and less humid \nair will be advected into our region with W/NW winds becoming breezy \n(gusts 25-35 mph Sunday and Monday). Generally drier conditions are \nthen expected for at least the first half of next week, although a \nfew moisture-starved upper disturbances embedded in NW flow aloft \nmay lead to some light showers for northern/high terrain areas \nMonday and again Tuesday night/early Wednesday. Temperatures \ngenerally remain near normal (highs in the 40s to 50s and lows in \nthe 20s to 30s) across the region for the first half of the week, \nbut deeper upper troughing over the eastern part of the country may \nlead to temperatures trending below normal by the end of next week.",
